 - **A scrub or parser slice measures a committed real-shape corpus before the red (experiment; card 439 retro, Decider D263).** While this entry is on trial: when a card changes a function that removes, rewrites or recognises text, the slice names and commits a real-shape corpus before the red: at least five shape families the function will meet in real output (for a path scrub: compiler and linker arguments, environment-variable lists such as PATH, container volume specs, passwd lines and JSON logs), each family with real inputs and their expected outputs; the architect measures the reference against it (no leak, no regression against the old version, a stable second pass, linear time), and the red commits it as rows.
   - Hypothesis: measuring real-shape families at the slice finds the design gaps review rounds otherwise find one per round. Metric: new BLOCKING findings after the first fix round on such cards, reported next to new MINOR (card 439: 2 and 3), target 2 or fewer. Counter-metric: minutes added to the slice (more than 20 minutes on a card is a miss). Trial: the next 3 cards that change such a function, counted from Decider D263. Owner: the facilitator. Review at the third such card's retro: keep, change or remove.
+  - Encodings and fix neighbours (card 441 retro, Decider D265): the corpus also holds at least one row for each input encoding the function will meet (for a path scrub: plain text, URI forms such as file: and jar:, JSON-escaped text, and doubled or mixed separators); and each fix slice commits rows for the neighbour variants of every rule it changes (its separators, escapes, case and one-letter forms) before that fix round's red. Metric: review BLOCKING findings whose shape had no committed row in its family, encoding or fix-neighbour set (card 441: 5 of 5), target 0, reported with D263's metric. Counter-metric: minutes added to each fix slice (more than 15 is a miss). Trial: inside D263's trial (the next 2 such cards), reviewed with it. Owner: the facilitator.
 - **Slice size cap.** Every slice file is 15360 bytes or fewer; a larger card is split into numbered slices (slice 1 of n), each its own commit carrying whole criteria, slice n+1 committed before the first red that needs it. The architect ends each slice with "Size: <N> bytes", N measured with `head -n -1 <slice> | wc -c`, and repeats N in the commit body; the reviewer re-measures with `git show <commit>:<path> | head -n -1 | wc -c` and checks it first; an oversize slice, a missing Size line or a mismatch counts as not run. Anything moved to a follow-up card to meet the cap is named in the slice commit body. The mid-card review and a read-marker for findings are deferred, not rejected.
   - Hypothesis: a slice size cap cuts review findings per card. Metric: review findings per card, slice bytes. Trial: 2 cards that would have a slice over 15 KB or three or more criteria (extended at the card 299 retro). Decider D168, started 2026-11-01.
 
